DTSTAMP:20261002T201848Z
DTSTART:20261004T130000Z
DTEND:20261004T134500Z
SUMMARY:Built in Chaos: Building and Scaling When the Rules Keep Changing
DESCRIPTION:In the MENA region\, chaos is not the exception\, it is the operating environment.\nFounders are building companies amid shifting markets\, disrupted supply chains\, and constant uncertainty. Yet many are not just surviving\, they are adapting faster\, making sharper decisions\, and finding unexpected opportunities inside disruption.\n“Built in Chaos” brings together founders and operators to share real\, unfiltered experiences of building and scaling in unstable conditions. The conversation goes beyond theory to focus on what actually works on the ground: how decisions are made under pressure\, how teams stay focused\, and how disruption can become a driver of growth.\nAt its core\, this panel explores one question: how do you build something lasting when nothing around you is stable?\n
CATEGORIES:STARTUP INNOVATION
LOCATION:(ALX) Stage 04: Techne Arena\, Egypt
SEQUENCE:0
UID:674fa2c675569cf53a3ba5bb96f8fbaf
URL:http://technesummit2026.sched.com/event/674fa2c675569cf53a3ba5bb96f8fbaf
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20261002T201848Z
DTSTART:20261004T133000Z
DTEND:20261004T141500Z
SUMMARY:Designing for People: The Intersection of Architecture and Human Behavior
DESCRIPTION:Architecture has the power to shape far more than the built environment\, it influences how people think\, feel\, interact\, and experience the world around them. From the flow of a workplace and the comfort of a healthcare facility to the atmosphere of a retail destination or public space\, every design decision has a direct impact on human behavior\, well-being\, and social interaction.\n\nThis session explores the intersection of architecture\, psychology\, and human-centered design\, examining how behavioral science\, neuroscience\, and environmental psychology are reshaping the way architects and designers approach the built environment. Panelists will discuss how elements such as spatial planning\, natural light\, biophilic design\, acoustics\, color\, and materiality can enhance wellness\, productivity\, creativity\, inclusivity\, and emotional connection.\n\nBringing together architects\, designers\, developers\, and behavioral experts\, the discussion will highlight how placing people at the center of the design process creates environments that are not only functional and aesthetically compelling\, but also healthier\, more engaging\, and better equipped to meet the evolving needs of modern communities.
CATEGORIES:ARCH. & REALESTATE
LOCATION:(ALX) Stage 02: Small Theater\, Egypt
